🚀 URL Shortener — Final Interview Notes (Complete + Perfect to Speak)
1. Problem Statement

“A URL shortener converts long URLs into short, unique codes and redirects users to the original URL with very low latency.”

2. Functional Requirements

Shorten a long URL → short code

Redirect short code → long URL

Optional: custom aliases, expiry, and click analytics

3. Non-Functional Requirements

High availability

Low latency (fast redirects)

Horizontal scalability

Unique short codes

Durability of stored mappings

4. Core APIs

POST /shorten → create short URL

GET /{code} → redirect to long URL

5. High-Level Architecture

“Requests flow through a Load Balancer to stateless App Servers, which use Redis Cache for fast lookups and a NoSQL database for persistent storage.”

Architecture Flow:
Client → Load Balancer → App Servers → Redis → NoSQL DB

6. Short Code Generation (Base62)

“I store the URL in the DB, get an auto-increment numeric ID, and convert it into a Base62 string (a–z, A–Z, 0–9). This gives compact, URL-safe, unique short codes.”

7. Database Choice (NoSQL Key-Value)

“I use a NoSQL key-value database like DynamoDB or Cassandra because lookups are simple and extremely read-heavy. Key = short code, value = long URL.”

8. Caching (Critical Section)

“Since redirects are 100× more frequent than writes, Redis caches shortCode → longURL to serve responses in microseconds and reduce DB load.”

9. Load Balancing

“A load balancer distributes traffic evenly across multiple app servers, improving scalability, reliability, and availability.”

10. Scalability

“The system scales horizontally because app servers are stateless.
For very large datasets, the database can be sharded by short code range to spread storage and query load.”

11. Collision Handling

“Auto-increment ID + Base62 encoding guarantees unique short codes, so collisions naturally never occur.”

12. Optional Enhancements

Link expiration (TTL)

Custom aliases

QR code generation

Click analytics

User dashboard

⭐ FINAL 30-SECOND INTERVIEW SUMMARY (Covers ALL Important Points)

“I’d design a URL shortener using stateless app servers behind a load balancer.
When a user submits a URL, I store it in a NoSQL key-value DB, take the auto-generated ID, and encode it using Base62 to create a short, unique code.
Redirects are extremely read-heavy, so I use Redis caching for microsecond lookup of shortCode-to-longURL mappings.
The system scales horizontally by adding more stateless app servers, and the database can be sharded by short code range for large-scale traffic.
Since IDs are unique, Base62 ensures no collisions. Optional features like custom aliases, TTL expiration, QR codes, and analytics can be added easily.”
